@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] exporter-is-generic
The exporter MUST hold only methods that serve every asset of their kind: retargeting, pose editing,
tiling, laying overlays, shared shape helpers. It MUST NOT name a particular asset (a character, an
animation, a region) or hold one asset's numbers, shapes, courses, colours or choices. Those belong in
that asset's `src/`, which calls the exporter's methods and helpers. A concept only one asset uses (a road,
a stance, a building) MUST NOT appear in the exporter until a second asset needs it, and then only as a
general method under a general name.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] exporter-lists-are-the-only-names
`rig/animations.rs`, `rig/characters.rs` and `world/regions.rs` are the only exporter files that name assets.
Each MUST do no more than compile in each asset's `.rs` file with `#[path]`, define the one trait every
asset of its kind implements (`Animation`, `Character`, `Region`), and list the assets. The exporter MUST
reach an asset only through that trait, never by matching on which asset it is, so adding an asset adds one
`#[path]` module and one list entry, and changes no other exporter file. An asset's folder name picks its
entry in the list.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] exports-follow-from-inputs
An export MUST depend only on its asset's settings, its `src/`, its kind's code (below) and the exports it
declares it reads, so that the same inputs give byte-identical files. The inputs checksum MUST cover all of
them, and the exporter MUST NOT read anything else. A read export is covered by its own recorded checksum and
its bytes, so a change to the code that made it reaches the assets that read it. Docs and `CONTRACTS` files are
never inputs. An export MAY check that an asset it names exists, without reading its files.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] each-kind-is-self-contained
Each kind's code MUST live in its own folder under `src/` (`rig/` for animations and characters, `texture/` for
textures, `model/` for plants and props, `world/` for the world) and use only that folder, the shared code
(`SHARED_CODE` in `main.rs`: `main.rs`, `glb.rs`, `shapes.rs`, `random.rs`, the crate's manifest, the lock file and
the toolchain) and its own asset groups' `.rs` files. It MUST NOT use another kind's code, and the shared code MUST
NOT use any kind's code, except where `main.rs` names each kind once to read its settings and hand its asset to it
(`Asset`); a method two kinds need moves into the shared code under a general name. A kind's checksum MUST cover
exactly that code, so editing one kind never marks another kind's assets out of date. Every file in `src/` MUST be
shared code or sit in a kind's folder. The exporter's tests check these rules, and CI runs them.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] an-edit-rebuilds-what-it-touches
An export MUST rebuild only the parts an edit touches: a world export rewrites only the tiles whose own checksum
changed. A tile's checksum MUST cover everything its files depend on (the code that writes tiles, the settings it
uses, the heights it reads, and every region's content that reaches into it, with the names of the textures and
models it shows and the anchors its cables fasten to) and nothing else, so an incremental export gives the same
files as a full one. The bytes of a tile MUST depend only on code in `world/` other than `regions.rs`, on the shared
code other than `main.rs` and on the locked crate versions. Exporter code for another kind MUST NOT change the
world's tiles, a region MUST NOT make tiles it does not reach rebuild, and repainting a texture or reshaping a model
MUST NOT rebuild any tile, though moving a prop's anchors rebuilds the tiles its cables start in.
